BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Accent Poetry Series: Garth Martens\, Loch Baillie\, David Gosseli
 n\, plus open mic!
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261018T113358
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261018T133358
DTSTAMP:20261018T210305Z
UID:a09f78d50fd042a404dbd277@montreal-poetry-calendar
CATEGORIES:Poetry & Literature
DESCRIPTION:Join poets Loch Baillie\, David Gosselin\, and Garth Martens a
 t Bar Le Record for an evening of poetry at the Accent Poetry Series\, a M
 ontreal-based literary series and open mic. The open mic follows the featu
 red readings.\n\nMontréal\, Québec — Accent Poetry Series Sunday\, Oct
 . 18\, 8:00–11:00 p.m. Bar Le Record 7622 rue Saint-Hubert\, Montréal\,
  QC H2R 2N6\n\nLOCH BAILLIE\n\nIn his highly anticipated\, breathtakingly 
 intimate debut\, Loch Baillie pens an unfiltered queer Bildungsroman that 
 traces both a geographical and emotional journey from his birthplace in Ne
 w England to where he comes to settle in Quebec.\n\nRaw and confessional\,
  River Running dives gracefully into the intangible concept of home\, be i
 t person or place. “No more metaphors tonight\,” writes Baillie\, “I
  come to tell you nothing / but the truth.” This collection probes at me
 mory and love\, identity and grief\, with achingly gentle hands\, sifting 
 through the fragments one both leaves behind and becomes.\n\nLoch Baillie 
 is a writer\, editor\, and teacher based in Québec. He is the author of t
 wo poetry chapbooks\, ice\, dove\, parachute (Cactus Press) and Citronella
  (Anstruther Press)\, as well as the full-length collection River Running 
 (Goose Lane Editions). His next book\, Head Split in Half\, doubles as his
  Master’s thesis at Université Laval. Loch currently serves as associat
 e poetry editor at Plenitude Magazine and as a board director for the Queb
 ec Writers’ Federation. Find him everywhere @lochbaillie.\n\nDAVID B. GO
 SSELIN\n\nDavid Gosselin is a writer\, researcher\, translator and filmmak
 er. He is the founder of the multimedia platform Age of Muses on Substack
 \, where he produces adaptations of both modern and classic works of poetr
 y and literature\, along with films\, stories and dialogues. He also runs 
 New Lyre Magazine\, the print organ.\n\nGARTH MARTENS\n\nIn his first coll
 ection since the Governor General’s–nominated Prologue for the Age of 
 Consequence\, Garth Martens adds an autobiographical lens to his concerns 
 about class and labour.\n\nA mother disappears. A son struggles to make se
 nse of the life she left behind. Set against the stark northern Alberta of
  the 1980s and 90s\, Who Else in the Dark Headed There follows a man reach
 ing through time to find the child he was and the father he is becoming. B
 eneath this waking world is another world\, of the overheard\, of the unsa
 id. To enter is to find a lyricism finely wrought and hallucinatory\, a de
 pth of feeling and fidelity to metaphor in all its guises—but most of al
 l an urgent relationship with language. Here\, in a reconstruction of chil
 dhood’s rooms\, Garth Martens approaches the past not as a record but as
  a pressure\, a “muscled concentration” that reorders\, resuscitates\,
  and redoubts.\n\nPoems of intense emotion\, the torque on the language ex
 treme—as it must be\, reaching this far into the dark\, this honestly. 
 —Jan Zwicky\n\nThis is hard stuff\, looked at with a cold eye. It’s be
 autiful\, fierce\, profoundly defensive\, smart as heck\, and intrusive. 
 —Harold Rhenisch\, The BC Review\n\nArresting fragments emerge from a re
 lentless invocation of half scenes\, stitched together with the singular l
 ogic of poetic memory (which is to say\, with mystery.) —Literary Review
  of Canada\n\nThe fragmented poems showcase an urgent relationship with la
 nguage that is sensory\, emotive and captivating. —CBC Books\n\nGarth Ma
 rtens is the author of Who Else in the Dark Headed There (Biblioasis\, 202
 6)\, the chapbook Remediation (JackPine\, 2018)\, and Prologue for the Age
  of Consequence (Anansi\, 2014)\, for which he was a finalist for the Gove
 rnor General’s Literary Award in Poetry. He is also a past winner of the
  Bronwen Wallace Award for Emerging Writers. His poetry appears in Dark Mo
 untain Project\, BODY Literature\, Poetry Ireland\, Hazlitt\, This Magazin
 e\, Fiddlehead\, Vallum\, Grain\, Prism\, Grain\, and Best Canadian Poetry
 . He is co-founder of Palabra Flamenco\, a literary flamenco ensemble that
  joins traditional flamenco dance and music with poetry and oral storytell
 ing. He lives on Vancouver Island. Find him on Instagram @garthmartens.\n
 \nACCENT POETRY SERIES\n\nEstablished in 2019 by Devon Gallant and Luc-Ant
 oine Chiasson\, the Accent Reading Series was founded out of a desire to p
 rovide Montreal authors with a reliable venue to congregate and share thei
 r work with one another. Infamous for our unlimited open mic spots and the
  dreaded five-minute bell\, over thirty open mic readers have graced the A
 ccent stage on any given night.\n\nListing: https://cultmtl.com/event/acce
 nt-poetry-series-garth-martens-loch-baillie-david-gosselin-plus-open-mic-2
 /\nMore information: https://cultmtl.com/event/accent-poetry-series-garth-
 martens-loch-baillie-david-gosselin-plus-open-mic-2/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://cultmtl.com/event/accent-poetry-series-garth-martens-loch-bail
 lie-david-gosselin-plus-open-mic-2/
X-MONTREAL-LITERARY-SOURCE:cult-mtl
END:VEVENT
